00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:22.981
MARK MANDEL: Let's do it!

00:03:22.981 --> 00:03:24.780
MELANIE WARRICK: This
week, we have with us

00:03:24.780 --> 00:03:26.610
two wonderful people
from Grace.health.

00:03:26.610 --> 00:03:28.740
We have Therese Mannheimer,
who's the founder,

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.570
and we have Roman Jasins.

00:03:30.570 --> 00:03:32.020
Thank you both for joining us.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:32.890
THERESE MANNHEIMER: Thank you.

00:03:32.890 --> 00:03:33.750
ROMAN JASINS: Thank you.

00:03:33.750 --> 00:03:34.710
MELANIE WARRICK:
So Therese, can you

00:03:34.710 --> 00:03:36.610
tell us a little
bit about yourself?

00:03:36.610 --> 00:03:37.860
THERESE MANNHEIMER: Yes, sure.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:41.850
I have a business background,
and have been an entrepreneur

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:44.700
the majority of my career,
starting different projects

00:03:44.700 --> 00:03:48.060
and also a foundation
working to drive opinion

00:03:48.060 --> 00:03:51.660
around female representation
in management teams and boards.

00:03:51.660 --> 00:03:55.740
But I've also spent around
seven years in a design agency,

00:03:55.740 --> 00:03:59.820
learning about design thinking,
business design strategy,

00:03:59.820 --> 00:04:04.230
and also how to really work with
real problems and challenges

00:04:04.230 --> 00:04:08.350
to be able to help more people
and build relevant services.

00:04:08.350 --> 00:04:09.600
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:11.280
And Roman, what about you?

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:14.230
ROMAN JASINS: I'm a technologist
with a technical background.

00:04:14.230 --> 00:04:18.029
So I've been helping people like
Therese building their dream

00:04:18.029 --> 00:04:20.760
products and
services, and working

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:23.100
very closely with engineers.

00:04:23.100 --> 00:04:25.380
Used to be an engineer
myself, way back.

00:04:25.380 --> 00:04:27.424
But then had to go
into management.

00:04:27.424 --> 00:04:28.590
MELANIE WARRICK: You had to.

00:04:28.590 --> 00:04:29.460
You were forced.

00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.260
ROMAN JASINS: But
I always enjoyed

00:04:31.260 --> 00:04:35.160
to triangulate between design,
product, and engineering.

00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.410
And that's where I think the
most fun is, when it comes down

00:04:38.410 --> 00:04:39.420
to development.

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:40.378
MELANIE WARRICK: Great.

00:04:40.378 --> 00:04:42.060
So Therese, I know
you're the founder.

00:04:42.060 --> 00:04:44.685
Roman, what is your role,
again, on Grace.health?

00:04:44.685 --> 00:04:47.120
ROMAN JASINS: I'm CTO,
and co-founder as well.

00:04:47.120 --> 00:04:48.790
MELANIE WARRICK:
Co-founder as well.

00:04:48.790 --> 00:04:51.582
And so how did y'all come up
with-- or what is Grace.health?

00:04:51.582 --> 00:04:53.040
THERESE MANNHEIMER:
We're a company

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:58.950
that wants to create change
worldwide and let women own

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:00.930
the information and data
about their own body,

00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.820
so that they can make
more informed decisions.

00:05:03.820 --> 00:05:06.510
And we have a grand
vision to really make

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:10.180
female health accessible to
all women across the globe.

00:05:10.180 --> 00:05:12.720
But we've broken it down
into steps to make it, like,

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:13.690
where do we start?

00:05:13.690 --> 00:05:16.164
And what type of issues
do we address first?

00:05:16.164 --> 00:05:17.580
And so the first
thing we're doing

00:05:17.580 --> 00:05:20.580
is building a what we'd
like to call female health

00:05:20.580 --> 00:05:24.900
companion that lets women
track and plan their period,

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:28.170
understand their fertility
and reproductive health,

00:05:28.170 --> 00:05:31.260
and then ask all the questions
that they want on what's

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:35.460
happening around their
bodies, sexuality, identity,

00:05:35.460 --> 00:05:36.900
and everything related.

00:05:36.900 --> 00:05:37.470
MELANIE WARRICK: That's great.

00:05:37.470 --> 00:05:39.303
Roman, was there anything
you wanted to add,

00:05:39.303 --> 00:05:42.026
in terms of the company
and the goals behind it?

00:05:42.026 --> 00:05:43.650
ROMAN JASINS: I think
this is very well

00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:44.700
summarized by Therese.

00:05:44.700 --> 00:05:46.560
And obviously, the
vision is global,

00:05:46.560 --> 00:05:50.160
so it needs to scale
globally, and be available

00:05:50.160 --> 00:05:54.002
literally for women in different
environments, different needs.

00:05:54.002 --> 00:05:55.710
THERESE MANNHEIMER:
I think what is worth

00:05:55.710 --> 00:05:59.280
adding is also that we
have looked at emerging

00:05:59.280 --> 00:06:01.740
markets as a very
interesting new market

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.612
to try and build
things for, since many

00:06:03.612 --> 00:06:05.070
of the products
out there are built

00:06:05.070 --> 00:06:07.770
for more westernized markets.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.430
But we don't intend to sort
of cut back on technology.

00:06:11.430 --> 00:06:15.060
We really want to build
high-end new technology

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:18.150
things with a very
lightweight interface.

00:06:18.150 --> 00:06:21.810
So it's still a very
high-tech company

00:06:21.810 --> 00:06:25.980
that we're building, and with
very smart and interesting

00:06:25.980 --> 00:06:26.610
integrations.

00:06:26.610 --> 00:06:28.860
MARK MANDEL: Was there a
particular catalyst for this?

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:30.693
Like, what was the
reasoning and the reasons

00:06:30.693 --> 00:06:32.680
behind when you
started this project?

00:06:32.680 --> 00:06:34.179
THERESE MANNHEIMER:
Both yes and no.

00:06:34.179 --> 00:06:37.200
I think, coming from a
business background just

00:06:37.200 --> 00:06:42.060
also had me have this more
economic perspective in mind.

00:06:42.060 --> 00:06:44.760
But then it was
really my background

00:06:44.760 --> 00:06:48.240
in a design agency
that helped me convert

00:06:48.240 --> 00:06:49.754
user insights into business.

00:06:49.754 --> 00:06:51.420
And that's also what
I was working with.

00:06:51.420 --> 00:06:55.920
So understanding how
to transform user needs

00:06:55.920 --> 00:06:58.500
and behaviors into real
products and services that

00:06:58.500 --> 00:07:01.980
could generate an income
and be a valid business

00:07:01.980 --> 00:07:04.500
was something that I
couldn't escape from.

00:07:04.500 --> 00:07:05.960
It just hit me really strongly.

00:07:05.960 --> 00:07:09.600
I want to solve real
issues for many people.

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:13.080
And then at the same time, I
was part of building Allbright,

00:07:13.080 --> 00:07:15.750
that I mentioned, which is a
foundation that really wants

00:07:15.750 --> 00:07:17.760
to talk about meritocracy.

00:07:17.760 --> 00:07:21.390
And so what we as women
can do, when we just

00:07:21.390 --> 00:07:25.330
have the best possible platform
to do it, is incredible.

00:07:25.330 --> 00:07:29.190
And so those two fields
just merged for me,

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.470
after leaving my previous job.

00:07:31.470 --> 00:07:34.650
And so I really wanted to see,
what if we take everything

00:07:34.650 --> 00:07:37.830
we know about this technology,
and make it much more

00:07:37.830 --> 00:07:39.240
accessible, and make it, like--

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:40.860
what if we scale it back down?

00:07:40.860 --> 00:07:44.040
So that was the
embryo for the idea.

00:07:44.040 --> 00:07:45.750
And then we went for it.

00:07:45.750 --> 00:07:48.510
And so yes, now we're building
a whole product and company

00:07:48.510 --> 00:07:49.660
around it.

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:50.550
MELANIE WARRICK:
And what's the goal,

00:07:50.550 --> 00:07:52.091
in terms of impact
that you're trying

00:07:52.091 --> 00:07:53.464
to have with this product?

00:07:53.464 --> 00:07:55.380
THERESE MANNHEIMER: There
are many, of course.

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:58.200
It's almost impossible
to just name a couple.

00:07:58.200 --> 00:08:01.950
But we want all women
to feel informed enough

00:08:01.950 --> 00:08:04.750
to make decisions
for themselves.

00:08:04.750 --> 00:08:07.200
We want all children who
are coming into this world

00:08:07.200 --> 00:08:07.990
to be wanted.

00:08:07.990 --> 00:08:12.060
And we hope to really make sure
that all women and girls can

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:15.060
go to school and
work, as they should,

00:08:15.060 --> 00:08:18.960
without ever having to be
sort of prevented by the fact

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.259
that they're women, and they
might be on their period.

00:08:21.259 --> 00:08:22.800
MELANIE WARRICK:
And actually, I just

00:08:22.800 --> 00:08:25.050
want to take a note of that,
from the standpoint of,

00:08:25.050 --> 00:08:27.466
for those who are listening
who may not know this, can you

00:08:27.466 --> 00:08:29.460
expand a little bit
more about that issue,

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:30.579
why that's an issue?

00:08:30.579 --> 00:08:32.370
THERESE MANNHEIMER: So
this is a huge issue

00:08:32.370 --> 00:08:36.090
that many, many, many
millions of women and girls

00:08:36.090 --> 00:08:40.559
around the globe are unable to
attend school and go to work,

00:08:40.559 --> 00:08:43.890
as they should, due to the
fact that there are stigmas

00:08:43.890 --> 00:08:47.010
and taboos around the world,
in different types of cultures,

00:08:47.010 --> 00:08:53.730
that tell us that women are
dirty, unclean, contagious,

00:08:53.730 --> 00:08:56.730
that you can catch
the demon, or many,

00:08:56.730 --> 00:08:59.910
all sorts of different
types of ideas

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.710
that are generated
from way back,

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.810
and that are not
founded in facts,

00:09:03.810 --> 00:09:07.200
but are really preventing
young girls and women to live

00:09:07.200 --> 00:09:09.150
their lives to the fullest.

00:09:09.150 --> 00:09:11.520
And it's not only
an efficiency issue

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.420
when it comes to girls and
women not going to school.

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.330
That affects, of course,
national economics

00:09:18.330 --> 00:09:22.320
and socioeconomics, that only
half the population can really

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:23.890
act and work to their fullest.

00:09:23.890 --> 00:09:27.330
But can you just imagine how
that must feel for someone,

00:09:27.330 --> 00:09:30.360
to feel dirty, or
to not be allowed

00:09:30.360 --> 00:09:33.260
to sleep inside the house
when you're menstruating,

00:09:33.260 --> 00:09:35.130
for something so
simple as actually

00:09:35.130 --> 00:09:36.152
being able to give life.

00:09:36.152 --> 00:09:37.110
MELANIE WARRICK: Right.

00:09:37.110 --> 00:09:39.151
THERESE MANNHEIMER: There's
a huge misconception.

00:09:39.151 --> 00:09:43.170
There's a huge miseducation,
disinformation, and also just

00:09:43.170 --> 00:09:46.080
fables, made-up
stuff, that we want

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:50.370
to break away from, and
really start to import,

00:09:50.370 --> 00:09:54.210
or give real access
to real knowledge,

00:09:54.210 --> 00:09:56.640
and sort of break away
from everything that has

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:58.171
to do with taboos and stigma.

00:09:58.171 --> 00:09:58.920
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:09:58.920 --> 00:10:01.005
OK, so you have this
project you're working on.

00:10:01.005 --> 00:10:02.880
But what is the project
that you're building,

00:10:02.880 --> 00:10:04.750
and how does it
combat these problems?

00:10:04.750 --> 00:10:06.333
THERESE MANNHEIMER:
So what we've done

00:10:06.333 --> 00:10:09.690
is that we have had an
early-stage pilot that we

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:10.950
launched in February.

00:10:10.950 --> 00:10:12.750
The embryo for
that was to really

00:10:12.750 --> 00:10:17.200
see if we can have girls
and women understand when

00:10:17.200 --> 00:10:21.310
their period is coming, and how
they can prepare for it best,

00:10:21.310 --> 00:10:23.680
to make sure they have a
collection method ready,

00:10:23.680 --> 00:10:26.710
to make sure they're mentally
and emotionally prepared.

00:10:26.710 --> 00:10:28.840
But also, I mean,
period is something

00:10:28.840 --> 00:10:30.670
that many women can feel.

00:10:30.670 --> 00:10:33.130
But ovulation and
fertility and when

00:10:33.130 --> 00:10:35.590
you can become pregnant
and not, is something

00:10:35.590 --> 00:10:37.750
that most women don't
know about, even

00:10:37.750 --> 00:10:39.070
in Western countries.

00:10:39.070 --> 00:10:39.520
MELANIE WARRICK: True.

00:10:39.520 --> 00:10:40.270
THERESE MANNHEIMER:
I didn't even

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:42.280
know about it before
I was pregnant,

00:10:42.280 --> 00:10:43.734
or I wanted to become pregnant.

00:10:43.734 --> 00:10:44.650
MELANIE WARRICK: True.

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:47.724
THERESE MANNHEIMER: And so
just understanding, oh, this

00:10:47.724 --> 00:10:49.390
is what's happening
in my body, and also

00:10:49.390 --> 00:10:53.290
being able to match symptoms
to what is, in fact, happening

00:10:53.290 --> 00:10:56.450
is super important to be able
to make informed decisions.

00:10:56.450 --> 00:11:00.310
So the idea was to make
something very lo-fi.

00:11:00.310 --> 00:11:03.910
And what we've done now is
we've created a service where

00:11:03.910 --> 00:11:07.930
you, inside of Facebook
Messenger, or Messenger Lite,

00:11:07.930 --> 00:11:12.490
currently, can use sort
of a conversational app.

00:11:12.490 --> 00:11:16.460
It's a chat bot that you talk
to, basically like a friend.

00:11:16.460 --> 00:11:18.820
And so we say, Hi,
I'm Grace.health.

00:11:18.820 --> 00:11:20.164
I'm a female health companion.

00:11:20.164 --> 00:11:21.580
I'm a specialist
in female health.

00:11:21.580 --> 00:11:24.610
And you can ask me anything
about your reproductive cycle

00:11:24.610 --> 00:11:25.810
or fertility.

00:11:25.810 --> 00:11:28.702
And then we start asking
you about, how old are you?

00:11:28.702 --> 00:11:29.410
What's your name?

00:11:29.410 --> 00:11:30.743
What do you want me to call you?

00:11:30.743 --> 00:11:32.230
When was your last period?

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:34.180
And we collect
the data manually,

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:37.540
but in a very conversational
and friendly way.

00:11:37.540 --> 00:11:40.210
And then we will ask
you about where you are,

00:11:40.210 --> 00:11:43.330
so that we can ping you timely.

00:11:43.330 --> 00:11:47.440
But then very much we'll have
proactive pings on our end.

00:11:47.440 --> 00:11:50.980
We will know, if we also
know how long your cycle is,

00:11:50.980 --> 00:11:54.910
when to inform you best, and
give you a heads-up in time,

00:11:54.910 --> 00:11:58.160
notify you around your
period and fertile period.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:02.140
And we will also send you
information about education.

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:04.230
Like, this is, if you're
tired at this point,

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:07.360
it can be due to hormonal
changes in your body.

00:12:07.360 --> 00:12:10.210
It's normal to feel tired.

00:12:10.210 --> 00:12:13.690
So we have a lot of proactive
pings and notifications.

00:12:13.690 --> 00:12:17.170
But then we also have
a very extensive set

00:12:17.170 --> 00:12:21.400
of contents that is reactive,
which means that if you ask me,

00:12:21.400 --> 00:12:23.260
why is this happening?

00:12:23.260 --> 00:12:25.090
I have tender breasts.

00:12:25.090 --> 00:12:27.250
What type of discharge
am I experiencing?

00:12:27.250 --> 00:12:28.600
Is this normal?

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:29.740
When can I get pregnant?

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:31.250
When is my fertile period?

00:12:31.250 --> 00:12:32.350
We can also respond.

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:36.040
So we are there, at
your convenience, 24/7,

00:12:36.040 --> 00:12:38.226
to also answer all
of your questions.

00:12:38.226 --> 00:12:39.100
MELANIE WARRICK: Wow.

00:12:39.100 --> 00:12:41.500
How did you build this?

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:43.720
ROMAN JASINS: So it's
what Therese already

00:12:43.720 --> 00:12:47.980
hinted, that there's quite an
extensive library of content,

00:12:47.980 --> 00:12:51.950
that is nuanced and relevant
in different contexts.

00:12:51.950 --> 00:12:55.900
So there is a back-end platform
that Facebook Messenger,

00:12:55.900 --> 00:12:59.710
and more messengers in the
future, integrate into.

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:04.630
And this is currently hosted
in Google Cloud, which kudos

00:13:04.630 --> 00:13:06.760
to you guys for building that.

00:13:06.760 --> 00:13:09.610
It makes our life
much easier, allows

00:13:09.610 --> 00:13:12.430
us to focus on the
core of the product,

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:15.520
instead of worrying about
scalability and infrastructure

00:13:15.520 --> 00:13:16.660
management.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:21.040
And we're storing all this
in quite dynamic database

00:13:21.040 --> 00:13:23.560
and model that is
changing as we learn

00:13:23.560 --> 00:13:26.470
and as we go along,
because in heart of it

00:13:26.470 --> 00:13:30.460
all is this predictive
algorithm, or model, that we

00:13:30.460 --> 00:13:33.490
coming up and trying,
because if you get

00:13:33.490 --> 00:13:35.560
the notifications
or the pings that we

00:13:35.560 --> 00:13:39.315
talked about in the wrong
time, then the value is lost.

00:13:39.315 --> 00:13:40.420
It needs to be timely.

00:13:40.420 --> 00:13:43.410
It needs to be in
the right chunk.

00:13:43.410 --> 00:13:46.660
And it needs to be served
in a way that is understood.

00:13:46.660 --> 00:13:48.640
Conversational UI
is great for this,

00:13:48.640 --> 00:13:50.980
because we're
using it every day,

00:13:50.980 --> 00:13:53.270
and everyone feels comfortable.

00:13:53.270 --> 00:13:56.560
But is also quite open,
open-ended, in the way

00:13:56.560 --> 00:13:58.520
that user ask questions.

00:13:58.520 --> 00:14:02.410
So it's easy, if you
get it done right.

00:14:02.410 --> 00:14:07.750
Otherwise, it could look quite
silly and unsophisticated.

00:14:07.750 --> 00:14:11.200
So there is an API,
obviously, that we built.

00:14:11.200 --> 00:14:15.790
And overall, setup is as
modular as we can keep it,

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:19.450
at this point, in order for
us to stay agile and nimble,

00:14:19.450 --> 00:14:24.280
and adjust and calibrate as we
get more data from the users.

00:14:24.280 --> 00:14:27.040
And then there is several
integration points

00:14:27.040 --> 00:14:30.360
through which we deliver
the conversation experience

00:14:30.360 --> 00:14:31.960
through things like Messenger.

00:14:31.960 --> 00:14:34.030
MARK MANDEL: Are you using
out-of-the-box tools,

00:14:34.030 --> 00:14:36.370
like something like Dialogflow,
or something like that?

00:14:36.370 --> 00:14:38.410
Or are you rolling your own?

00:14:38.410 --> 00:14:39.589
Or how is that working?

00:14:39.589 --> 00:14:40.630
ROMAN JASINS: It's a mix.

00:14:40.630 --> 00:14:43.060
We are heavily
relying on Dialogflow.

00:14:43.060 --> 00:14:46.600
And it helps us with
natural language processing

00:14:46.600 --> 00:14:47.770
and many other things.

00:14:47.770 --> 00:14:51.490
But since quite a lot
of what we're doing

00:14:51.490 --> 00:14:53.500
doesn't exist today--

00:14:53.500 --> 00:14:57.190
there is no tools that
predict your next period,

00:14:57.190 --> 00:15:00.520
or take all the variables
into the account--

00:15:00.520 --> 00:15:04.080
we had to build, and are
building, our own back end

00:15:04.080 --> 00:15:07.450
and libraries and APIs for
that, which we integrate.

00:15:07.450 --> 00:15:10.180
So if you would look
at how it's set up,

00:15:10.180 --> 00:15:13.840
there are certain things that
we get through Dialogflow,

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:15.685
or through Google tooling.

00:15:15.685 --> 00:15:19.150
But there are certain things
that we get directly and then

00:15:19.150 --> 00:15:22.180
send to Google, to
Dialogflow, in order

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:24.910
to interpret it properly,
and then get it back

00:15:24.910 --> 00:15:26.687
and send it back to the user.

00:15:26.687 --> 00:15:29.020
MELANIE WARRICK: What are
some of the biggest challenges

00:15:29.020 --> 00:15:31.690
you have with the UI
that you're building?

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:35.830
ROMAN JASINS: It's the fact
that it is so free for the user.

00:15:35.830 --> 00:15:39.230
There is a freedom of choice, so
how you interact, what you say,

00:15:39.230 --> 00:15:40.420
how you say it.

00:15:40.420 --> 00:15:45.160
Constraining people with menus,
or users with menus, and links,

00:15:45.160 --> 00:15:47.340
takes a lot of headache away.

00:15:47.340 --> 00:15:50.710
But when you have to expect
unexpected, and handle it

00:15:50.710 --> 00:15:53.480
in a way where it makes
sense to the user,

00:15:53.480 --> 00:15:54.990
that's quite challenging.

00:15:54.990 --> 00:15:57.340
And it's also very rewarding
when you get it right.

00:15:57.340 --> 00:16:00.580
So language processing,
natural language processing,

00:16:00.580 --> 00:16:02.320
and machine learning
is something

00:16:02.320 --> 00:16:05.550
that we breathe
and live every day.

00:16:05.550 --> 00:16:08.800
Scalability, of course,
something that we are aware

00:16:08.800 --> 00:16:10.190
and keeping top of the mind.

00:16:10.190 --> 00:16:12.700
But again, the
choices that we made,

00:16:12.700 --> 00:16:16.000
going with Google Platform,
also based on the fact

00:16:16.000 --> 00:16:19.910
that Google helps us
with scaling that part.

00:16:19.910 --> 00:16:21.620
So this is less of
a concern for us.

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:26.140
But then again, since it's
a global service, of course,

00:16:26.140 --> 00:16:28.630
it will be an issue as
we scale as a company.

00:16:28.630 --> 00:16:31.030
It's the cultural
context, and how

00:16:31.030 --> 00:16:34.660
one thing that we
say or we hear means

00:16:34.660 --> 00:16:38.090
something else in a different
region of the world.

00:16:38.090 --> 00:16:39.970
This is more and more
becoming apparent.

00:16:39.970 --> 00:16:43.300
And we're trying to find
interesting and creative ways

00:16:43.300 --> 00:16:44.640
to handling that.

00:16:44.640 --> 00:16:46.265
THERESE MANNHEIMER:
Can I also add that

00:16:46.265 --> 00:16:49.390
when it comes to UI, since
you asked about UIs also,

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:55.060
that we have a UI at the moment
that is without visualization.

00:16:55.060 --> 00:16:59.530
And visualization-- like
diagrams, infographics,

00:16:59.530 --> 00:17:01.930
anything that you can rest
your eye on, that has sort

00:17:01.930 --> 00:17:04.930
of a self-explanatory purpose--

00:17:04.930 --> 00:17:06.310
we don't have at the moment.

00:17:06.310 --> 00:17:08.530
We will have, but
we have to rely

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:10.599
on super intuitive content.

00:17:10.599 --> 00:17:14.560
We need to be super timely with
our pings and notifications.

00:17:14.560 --> 00:17:18.220
And so we're basically
resting on having

00:17:18.220 --> 00:17:22.869
enough skills to really tell you
the same story without having

00:17:22.869 --> 00:17:26.119
support of any type of images.

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:29.350
And that's where the
tonality comes in,

00:17:29.350 --> 00:17:32.380
and the timings are
super important.

00:17:32.380 --> 00:17:35.350
But we are, of
course, also looking

00:17:35.350 --> 00:17:39.951
into how we can combine text
with visual stimuli going

00:17:39.951 --> 00:17:40.450
forward.

00:17:40.450 --> 00:17:42.290
But it's an experimental effort.

00:17:42.290 --> 00:17:44.100
But of course it's
super challenging

00:17:44.100 --> 00:17:45.990
when we're only relying on text.

00:17:45.990 --> 00:17:46.740
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:17:46.740 --> 00:17:49.210
Roman, you mentioned
scalability, and how Google

00:17:49.210 --> 00:17:50.794
Cloud's helping you with that.

00:17:50.794 --> 00:17:52.210
Can you dig a
little deeper there?

00:17:52.210 --> 00:17:54.376
I'm curious to hear, like,
you mentioned Dialogflow.

00:17:54.376 --> 00:17:56.980
Are there other particular
products that you're using that

00:17:56.980 --> 00:17:59.110
maybe are the more, like,
platform-as-a-service,

00:17:59.110 --> 00:18:01.443
or anything like that, that
are really helping you solve

00:18:01.443 --> 00:18:02.590
the scalability problems?

00:18:02.590 --> 00:18:03.631
ROMAN JASINS: Absolutely.

00:18:03.631 --> 00:18:05.830
We're storing data in Firebase.

00:18:05.830 --> 00:18:09.820
We relying on Google
functions, and generally,

00:18:09.820 --> 00:18:13.720
try to use as many managed
solutions that you have.

00:18:13.720 --> 00:18:17.590
Not always possible, because we
have our own needs that might

00:18:17.590 --> 00:18:20.150
not be supported at the time.

00:18:20.150 --> 00:18:24.790
So then we default to
compute instances and run

00:18:24.790 --> 00:18:26.670
our own solutions on it.

00:18:26.670 --> 00:18:29.920
In-memory store could be an
example of something like this.

00:18:29.920 --> 00:18:33.940
But yeah, basically the
entire stack, top to bottom,

00:18:33.940 --> 00:18:36.742
we keep as much as
possible in Google Cloud.

00:18:36.742 --> 00:18:38.950
MELANIE WARRICK: And I
imagine getting the data, too,

00:18:38.950 --> 00:18:40.210
is its own challenge.

00:18:40.210 --> 00:18:43.300
How are you engaging
with community?

00:18:43.300 --> 00:18:45.040
Or are there ways
for, if there's

00:18:45.040 --> 00:18:47.290
others who are interested
in contributing to this,

00:18:47.290 --> 00:18:49.117
for them to engage with you?

00:18:49.117 --> 00:18:51.200
THERESE MANNHEIMER: That's
a really good question.

00:18:51.200 --> 00:18:53.980
And at the moment,
we are very much

00:18:53.980 --> 00:18:55.630
focused on building
the core product.

00:18:55.630 --> 00:18:58.760
But the scalability
of these data sets,

00:18:58.760 --> 00:19:01.150
and the interest in them,
are of course something

00:19:01.150 --> 00:19:02.470
that's driving us a lot.

00:19:02.470 --> 00:19:06.130
And so how not only
do we contribute,

00:19:06.130 --> 00:19:08.380
or how we collaborate
with others when

00:19:08.380 --> 00:19:10.960
they want to contribute to our
data set, but also how we can

00:19:10.960 --> 00:19:14.620
collaborate and
contribute to research,

00:19:14.620 --> 00:19:17.410
and to be part of
clinical trials

00:19:17.410 --> 00:19:20.140
that actually can
help build smarter

00:19:20.140 --> 00:19:22.180
services and products
and medicines

00:19:22.180 --> 00:19:25.090
for women relating
to female health.

00:19:25.090 --> 00:19:27.700
But all of this is yet to come.

00:19:27.700 --> 00:19:30.490
And we are in some
early discussions

00:19:30.490 --> 00:19:33.430
with really interesting
partners, but nothing that I

00:19:33.430 --> 00:19:35.530
can disclose at this moment.

00:19:35.530 --> 00:19:36.880
MELANIE WARRICK: That's fair.

00:19:36.880 --> 00:19:38.980
My understanding is you're
part of the startup program

00:19:38.980 --> 00:19:39.605
through Google.

00:19:39.605 --> 00:19:40.370
Is that right?

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:41.240
THERESE MANNHEIMER:
Yeah, that's right.

00:19:41.240 --> 00:19:43.000
MELANIE WARRICK: So
how has that been?

00:19:43.000 --> 00:19:44.140
What kind of support
have you received

00:19:44.140 --> 00:19:45.181
from the startup program?

00:19:45.181 --> 00:19:46.390
What has that been like?

00:19:46.390 --> 00:19:49.450
THERESE MANNHEIMER: Initially,
we had a contact with Daniel

00:19:49.450 --> 00:19:54.430
at Google, who has been
super helpful with making

00:19:54.430 --> 00:19:55.270
introductions.

00:19:55.270 --> 00:19:57.880
Of course, we were
granted credits

00:19:57.880 --> 00:19:59.830
that have been
very useful for us,

00:19:59.830 --> 00:20:04.600
since we're a bootstrapping,
or a very cash-light entity,

00:20:04.600 --> 00:20:06.390
and trying to really save.

00:20:06.390 --> 00:20:09.580
Of course, credits have been
very valuable for us now,

00:20:09.580 --> 00:20:10.540
setting up.

00:20:10.540 --> 00:20:14.800
But also, Daniel has been
incredibly useful and helpful

00:20:14.800 --> 00:20:17.760
in connecting us to
the right resources,

00:20:17.760 --> 00:20:21.150
enabling smart collaborations
with different entities

00:20:21.150 --> 00:20:23.620
within your organization.

00:20:23.620 --> 00:20:26.100
But of course I think
we have still yet

00:20:26.100 --> 00:20:29.970
to explore to the full
capabilities of this program.

00:20:29.970 --> 00:20:31.959
I think there is much
more we can make use of.

00:20:31.959 --> 00:20:33.000
ROMAN JASINS: Absolutely.

00:20:33.000 --> 00:20:35.960
If I can add to that,
having access to the brains

00:20:35.960 --> 00:20:40.890
and to the experience that
engineers and designers

00:20:40.890 --> 00:20:44.130
and developers at Google
have is instrumental for us,

00:20:44.130 --> 00:20:47.730
because we coming up with
something that we haven't

00:20:47.730 --> 00:20:50.700
seen yet done elsewhere.

00:20:50.700 --> 00:20:53.730
I mean, the chat bots,
or conversational UI,

00:20:53.730 --> 00:20:56.610
have been picking
up, and been solving

00:20:56.610 --> 00:20:59.550
quite interesting problems,
from customer service

00:20:59.550 --> 00:21:04.590
to automation of different
kind of mundane tasks.

00:21:04.590 --> 00:21:09.810
But we are trying to create a
real, meaningful conversation

00:21:09.810 --> 00:21:14.580
where you don't necessarily
perform linear tasks.

00:21:14.580 --> 00:21:17.910
You sometimes just need
to talk and find out

00:21:17.910 --> 00:21:21.720
whether what you're
experiencing is normal or not.

00:21:21.720 --> 00:21:24.360
So creating something
that doesn't

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:27.360
break in those scenarios
is not trivial.

00:21:27.360 --> 00:21:31.770
And we often feel that what
we trying to design actually

00:21:31.770 --> 00:21:35.430
is not mainstream, and may be
even far from being mainstream,

00:21:35.430 --> 00:21:37.350
might not even be
possible for a while.

00:21:37.350 --> 00:21:40.620
So having someone that
you can bounce this with,

00:21:40.620 --> 00:21:44.070
and having dialogue, is
very important for informing

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:47.970
our road map, what we
focus on, not just solving

00:21:47.970 --> 00:21:50.660
specific problems, the
technical problems, that we're

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:51.790
facing today.

00:21:51.790 --> 00:21:54.970
So we would love to do
more, as much as possible,

00:21:54.970 --> 00:21:56.804
of this kind of interactions.

00:21:56.804 --> 00:21:57.720
MELANIE WARRICK: Nice.

00:21:57.720 --> 00:22:00.390
Well, I want to take it to
one other question that's

00:22:00.390 --> 00:22:02.282
a little different,
but similar, in terms

00:22:02.282 --> 00:22:04.365
of your data and the data
that you're working with

00:22:04.365 --> 00:22:06.240
and the community
you're working with.

00:22:06.240 --> 00:22:09.300
How are you helping to
ensure their, I guess,

00:22:09.300 --> 00:22:13.140
privacy of information,
if they want that?

00:22:13.140 --> 00:22:15.600
ROMAN JASINS: First of
all, we try to never ask

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:17.400
something we don't need.

00:22:17.400 --> 00:22:20.130
User is always in focus,
and we only ask for things

00:22:20.130 --> 00:22:23.670
that we need to have
in order to be precise,

00:22:23.670 --> 00:22:27.090
to be relevant, and
calibrate for the user.

00:22:27.090 --> 00:22:31.440
Then, obviously, even though
we process and store the data,

00:22:31.440 --> 00:22:32.850
it is user data.

00:22:32.850 --> 00:22:34.440
And if for some
reason they decide

00:22:34.440 --> 00:22:36.660
to discontinue
using Grace, that's

00:22:36.660 --> 00:22:41.400
the choice that we will respect,
and remove or release data

00:22:41.400 --> 00:22:42.630
