00:28:59.673 --> 00:29:01.920
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
Mark, with background cloud

00:29:01.920 --> 00:29:04.770
functions, how can I set
up to retry on failure?

00:29:04.770 --> 00:29:06.280
MARK MANDEL: Great question!

00:29:06.280 --> 00:29:07.220
MELANIE WARRICK: I know,
it's such a good question.

00:29:07.220 --> 00:29:08.010
MARK MANDEL: That's
such a good question.

00:29:08.010 --> 00:29:08.620
MELANIE WARRICK:
It's so amazing.

00:29:08.620 --> 00:29:10.745
MARK MANDEL: So if you're
running a cloud function,

00:29:10.745 --> 00:29:13.710
but you want it to respond to
something like a Google Cloud

00:29:13.710 --> 00:29:16.110
Pub/Sub topic, or a change
in Google Cloud Storage

00:29:16.110 --> 00:29:17.717
Bucket, or maybe
a Firebase event,

00:29:17.717 --> 00:29:19.550
you're going to write
a background function.

00:29:19.550 --> 00:29:21.614
But maybe that
background function talks

00:29:21.614 --> 00:29:23.280
to something that's
intermittently down.

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:24.900
Like, maybe there's
a database that

00:29:24.900 --> 00:29:26.525
needs to go into
maintenance sometimes,

00:29:26.525 --> 00:29:28.709
or a third-party API
that needs to go down,

00:29:28.709 --> 00:29:30.750
or isn't necessarily as
stable as you would like.

00:29:30.750 --> 00:29:32.458
So you want to make
sure that these retry

00:29:32.458 --> 00:29:34.134
until basically they pass.

00:29:34.134 --> 00:29:35.800
This is actually
pretty straightforward.

00:29:35.800 --> 00:29:37.740
So when you deploy your
background function,

00:29:37.740 --> 00:29:40.230
you can basically add a
dash dash retry to it.

00:29:40.230 --> 00:29:42.540
You can do the same thing
for the console as well.

00:29:42.540 --> 00:29:44.670
And that will retry
until it succeeds.

00:29:44.670 --> 00:29:46.950
You should probably be
aware that retry on failure

00:29:46.950 --> 00:29:49.410
does cause your function
to be retried repeatedly

00:29:49.410 --> 00:29:51.110
until it successfully executes.

00:29:51.110 --> 00:29:52.110
MELANIE WARRICK: And
you don't want to end up

00:29:52.110 --> 00:29:53.430
with infinity retry loops.

00:29:53.430 --> 00:29:55.410
MARK MANDEL: Yep, or
the maximum retry period

00:29:55.410 --> 00:29:57.430
has elapsed, which
can be multiple days.

00:29:57.430 --> 00:29:59.079
So if it's a bug or
anything like that,

00:29:59.079 --> 00:30:00.120
you'll need to delete it.

00:30:00.120 --> 00:30:02.807
But make sure that you test
your stuff before you do stuff.

00:30:02.807 --> 00:30:04.140
But it's pretty straightforward.

00:30:04.140 --> 00:30:07.020
It's a really easy thing to
just be able say, hey, retry.

00:30:07.020 --> 00:30:09.120
You don't have to build
any logic into your code.

00:30:09.120 --> 00:30:10.329
It will just do that for you.

00:30:10.329 --> 00:30:12.953
MELANIE WARRICK: And if you want
to avoid infinity retry loops,

00:30:12.953 --> 00:30:14.830
you can set an end
condition to avoid that.

00:30:14.830 --> 00:30:15.240
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:30:15.240 --> 00:30:15.360
Yes.

00:30:15.360 --> 00:30:16.420
That's a good idea too.

00:30:16.420 --> 00:30:16.710
Awesome.

00:30:16.710 --> 00:30:17.585
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes.

00:30:17.585 --> 00:30:18.840
It's good best practice.

00:30:18.840 --> 00:30:19.670
